From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 01:59:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nvk/rm: small system memory allocations share pinned slabs

Every system memory allocation on Windows is our own pages registered
with RM as an OS descriptor: one pin in the Helios KMD, which allows at
most 256 per process, and a table of page runs the host maps one by one.
NVK makes many small ones (64 KiB command buffer chunks, descriptor pools,
query pools, upload streams).  Counter-Strike 2 on its Vulkan renderer
went past the pin quota (the KMD counted 15950 refused pins); the
fallback, RM's own system memory, needs a KMD handle for each CPU mapping
and ran out of those next (3359 refusals), and vkCreateGraphicsPipelines
and vkCreateDescriptorPool failed with VK_ERROR_MEMORY_MAP_FAILED.

- Allocations of up to 512 KiB that are system memory, not shared, not
  tiled and not RM-named (NVKMD_RM_MEM_RM_SYSMEM) are ranges of 4 MiB
  slabs, each one registration: GPU mappings use the slab's RM object at
  the range's offset, the CPU mapping is the slab's pages at that offset,
  frees return the range (one empty slab is kept).  A reused range is
  zeroed, as fresh pages were.  A slab that cannot be registered falls
  back to the allocation's own memory as before, and no new slab is
  tried for a second after such a refusal.  Slabs are registered outside
  the slab lock.
- A range has no RM object of its own: nvkmd_rm_mem_rm_handles() gives
  none for it (the Helios copy path then declines, as exports already
  did for memory backed by our own pages).
- NVK_RM_SYSMEM_SLABS=0 turns it off.
- vkCreateDescriptorPool initializes the pool's heap before anything can
  fail: its error path destroyed the pool and finished a heap that was
  never initialized (the access violation that followed the failures).
---
